item.text:
Chapter 2B research completed for Andrés Manuel López Obrador, Mexico, 2022. No score assigned and no other chapter researched.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Gobernar no debe ser hegemonía, sino búsqueda de bienestar para los pueblos","publisher":"Presidencia de la República de México","publication_date":"2022-09-16","url":"https://www.gob.mx/amlo/prensa/gobernar-no-debe-ser-hegemonia-sino-busqueda-de-bienestar-para-los-pueblos-presidente","claim":"López Obrador proposed a five-year truce and an international Committee for Dialogue and Peace to stop the Russia-Ukraine war.","locator":"Paragraphs 57-70","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-001","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.gob.mx/amlo/prensa/gobernar-no-debe-ser-hegemonia-sino-busqueda-de-bienestar-para-los-pueblos-presidente|57-70|peace committee proposal","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"],"source_type":"official_statement","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential statement, but self-interested and describing a proposal rather than an achieved settlement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly concerns September 2022 conduct.","ruler_attribution":"The proposal was publicly announced and routed through the foreign minister by the president.","contrary_evidence":["The proposal did not produce a documented ceasefire or settlement.","The statement also blamed NATO and Western sanctions in ways criticized as potentially one-sided."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.6","2B.7","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Intervención del Secretario de Relaciones Exteriores en el 77º período de sesiones de la Asamblea General","publisher":"Secretaría de Relaciones Exteriores de México","publication_date":"2022-09-22","url":"https://mision.sre.gob.mx/onu/images/2022/Destacados/COT_77_AGONU_-_Intervencion_del_Canciller_220922.pdf","claim":"Mexico, following López Obrador’s proposal, advocated confidence-building measures, a complementary diplomatic channel, mediation, and peaceful resolution in Ukraine.","locator":"PDF pages 1-2, paragraphs beginning “Por tales razones” through “diplomacia preventiva,” lines 45-70","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-002","canonical_fact_key":"https://mision.sre.gob.mx/onu/images/2022/Destacados/COT_77_AGONU_-_Intervencion_del_Canciller_220922.pdf|1-2|Mexico diplomatic channel Ukraine","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"],"source_type":"official_diplomatic_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official contemporaneous diplomatic record with precise date and text.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Delivered on 22 September 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The speech expressly identifies the proposal as originating with President López Obrador.","contrary_evidence":["The initiative did not demonstrably achieve negotiations or a ceasefire during the target period.","Mexico’s neutral posture created criticism from Ukraine and the United States."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"México: El traspaso de la Guardia Nacional a la Secretaría de la Defensa Nacional es un retroceso","publisher":"United Nations Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights","publication_date":"2022-09-09","url":"https://hchr.org.mx/comunicados/mexico-el-traspaso-de-la-guardia-nacional-a-la-secretaria-de-la-defensa-nacional-es-un-retroceso-para-la-seguridad-publica-basada-en-los-derechos-humanos/","claim":"The UN human-rights office said transferring the National Guard to Defense consolidated militarization, lacked effective civilian supervision, and was not shown to reduce crime sustainably.","locator":"Paragraphs 73-79","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-003","canonical_fact_key":"https://hchr.org.mx/comunicados/mexico-el-traspaso-de-la-guardia-nacional-a-la-secretaria-de-la-defensa-nacional-es-un-retroceso-para-la-seguridad-publica-basada-en-los-derechos-humanos/|73-79|National Guard transfer civilian oversight","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.3","2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"intergovernmental_human_rights_statement","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous UN assessment addressing the specific 2022 reform.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly concerns the September 2022 legislative transfer.","ruler_attribution":"The reform was part of the president’s security-policy agenda and executive initiative, while Congress formally enacted it.","contrary_evidence":["The statement recognizes that militarization predated López Obrador and that the reform was enacted by Congress.","The government presented military involvement as necessary for public security."],"lenses":["2B.3","2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"México: La militarización de la seguridad pública amenaza los derechos humanos","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-08-26","url":"https://www.hrw.org/es/news/2022/08/26/mexico-la-militarizacion-de-la-seguridad-publica-amenaza-los-derechos-humanos","claim":"HRW reported that López Obrador planned to formalize and extend military control of federal public-security tasks, despite longstanding allegations of abuses and impunity.","locator":"Paragraphs 416-438","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-004","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.hrw.org/es/news/2022/08/26/mexico-la-militarizacion-de-la-seguridad-publica-amenaza-los-derechos-humanos|430-438|military public security plan","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.3","2B.4","2B.5","2B.8","2B.9"],"source_type":"ngo_human_rights_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed contemporaneous documentation with specific policy actions and alleged consequences.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published during the August 2022 reform initiative.","ruler_attribution":"The report attributes the plan directly to López Obrador and identifies his administration’s proposed legal changes.","contrary_evidence":["HRW is an advocacy organization and emphasizes adverse rights evidence.","The government argued that military deployment supported security and public order."],"lenses":["2B.3","2B.4","2B.5","2B.8","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"México: Militarizar la seguridad pública generará más violaciones de derechos humanos","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-09-09","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/es/latest/news/2022/09/mexico-militarizing-public-security-human-rights-violations-impunity/","claim":"Amnesty reported that the reforms proposed by López Obrador placed the National Guard under Defense, expanded military staffing and training, and raised risks of military rather than civilian accountability; it cited more than 1,100 complaints against the Guard from 2020-2022.","locator":"Paragraphs 67-82","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-005","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.amnesty.org/es/latest/news/2022/09/mexico-militarizing-public-security-human-rights-violations-impunity/|67-82|National Guard militarization complaints","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.8","2B.9"],"source_type":"ngo_human_rights_statement","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous organization statement with concrete legislative details and complaint counts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly addresses the August-September 2022 reforms and 2020-2022 complaints.","ruler_attribution":"The statement identifies López Obrador’s 31 August proposal and the executive’s security-policy direction.","contrary_evidence":["The complaint total concerns the institution and does not establish that López Obrador personally ordered each alleged abuse.","Congress and courts remained separate institutional actors."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.8","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Annual Report 2022, Chapter V: Mexico","publisher":"Inter-American Commission on Human Rights","publication_date":"2023-04-01","url":"https://www.oas.org/en/iachr/docs/annual/2022/Chapters/14-IA2022_Cap_5_MX_EN.pdf","claim":"The IACHR recorded Mexico’s reported National Guard human-rights training while maintaining that the recommendation to strengthen civilian security and reduce militarization remained pending.","locator":"PDF pages 4-8, especially paragraphs 16-22 and lines 228-284","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-006","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.oas.org/en/iachr/docs/annual/2022/Chapters/14-IA2022_Cap_5_MX_EN.pdf|4-8|National Guard training pending demilitarization","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.3","2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"intergovernmental_annual_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Formal regional human-rights monitoring report distinguishing state claims from the Commission’s assessment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Reviews developments and state reporting through 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Concerns the federal security strategy pursued during López Obrador’s presidency, while recognizing institutional implementation by the Guard and other agencies.","contrary_evidence":["The state reported extensive human-rights and use-of-force training.","The report is a follow-up document published in 2023, so some findings summarize rather than contemporaneously observe 2022 events."],"lenses":["2B.3","2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico 2022 Human Rights Report","publisher":"U.S. Department of State, Bureau of Democracy, Human Rights, and Labor","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://www.theadvocatesforhumanrights.org/res/USDOS%2BHuman%2BRights%2BReport%2BMexico%2B2022.pdf","claim":"The report states that the National Guard was formally transferred to Defense on 16 September 2022, that military personnel formed its majority, and that credible reports involved unlawful killings, disappearances, torture, arbitrary detention, and low accountability.","locator":"PDF pages 0-1, Executive Summary and Section 1(a), lines 10-56","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-007","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.theadvocatesforhumanrights.org/res/USDOS%2BHuman%2BRights%2BReport%2BMexico%2B2022.pdf|0-1|National Guard transfer abuses accountability","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"government_human_rights_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual country report by a government human-rights bureau with explicit target-year coverage and detailed allegations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Specifically covers Mexico during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Documents executive security structures and presidential authority while distinguishing abuses by security personnel and prosecutors.","contrary_evidence":["The report says authorities generally maintained effective control over security forces, though some elements acted independently.","Criminal organizations, not only state forces, were major sources of violence."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Freedom in the World 2022 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2022-02-24","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/mexico/freedom-world/2022","claim":"Freedom House described severe rule-of-law deficits, violence by criminal and state actors, weak accountability for military abuses, and the López Obrador administration’s increasing reliance on militarized forces.","locator":"Sections C2-C3, D1, and F3; paragraphs at lines 151-165 and 227-236","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-008","canonical_fact_key":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/mexico/freedom-world/2022|C3-F3|militarization accountability violence","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9","2B.10"],"source_type":"ngo_country_report","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed independent country assessment, but the 2022 edition principally reports on 2021 conditions and is therefore contextual for this period.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Primarily inherited/pre-2022 conditions; useful for baseline and trajectory, not decisive target-year evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Attributes expansion of militarized public security to López Obrador while describing broader inherited institutional and criminal conditions.","contrary_evidence":["Most cited events predate 2022.","The report also notes Mexico’s competitive electoral system and some government efforts to respond to victims."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"AMLO’s Ukraine position is straining relations between Mexico City and Washington","publisher":"Le Monde","publication_date":"2022-03-29","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2022/03/29/amlo-s-ukraine-position-is-straining-relations-between-mexico-city-and-washington_5979305_4.html","claim":"Le Monde reported that López Obrador condemned Russia’s invasion and supported a UN resolution demanding an end to the war, but refused weapons and economic reprisals, declared neutrality, and faced criticism over a Mexico-Russia friendship committee involving allied legislators.","locator":"Paragraphs 210-240","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-009","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2022/03/29/amlo-s-ukraine-position-is-straining-relations-between-mexico-city-and-washington_5979305_4.html|232-240|Ukraine neutrality Mexico-Russia committee","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.1","2B.6","2B.10"],"source_type":"international_press_analysis","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous international reporting with direct quotations and diplomatic context, but not a primary legal or governmental record.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published during the March 2022 invasion and concerns immediate 2022 diplomacy.","ruler_attribution":"Directly reports López Obrador’s stated policy and distinguishes it from allied legislators’ actions.","contrary_evidence":["Mexico’s neutrality was consistent with its stated non-intervention tradition.","The article reports criticism and alleged Russian influence claims that were not independently established in the article."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.6","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Homicidio doloso, secuestro y robo de combustible a la baja al cierre de 2022","publisher":"Presidencia de la República de México","publication_date":"2023-01-17","url":"https://amlo.presidente.gob.mx/homicidio-doloso-secuestro-y-robo-de-combustible-a-la-baja-al-cierre-de-2022/","claim":"The government reported a decline in homicide victims in 2022 and credited coordinated armed-forces, security-cabinet, and peace-table work, while continuing large National Guard and military deployments.","locator":"Paragraphs 26-46","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-010","canonical_fact_key":"https://amlo.presidente.gob.mx/homicidio-doloso-secuestro-y-robo-de-combustible-a-la-baja-al-cierre-de-2022/|26-46|2022 homicide decline military deployment","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.3","2B.8","2B.10"],"source_type":"official_security_report","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Official contemporaneous statistics and attribution, but incentive exists to present the security strategy favorably and the page was published after year-end.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Reports full-year 2022 outcomes, published January 2023.","ruler_attribution":"The report links outcomes to the president’s security cabinet and national strategy.","contrary_evidence":["Independent human-rights reports document continuing abuses, disappearances, and impunity.","The report presents government-selected indicators and does not establish causality."],"lenses":["2B.3","2B.8","2B.10"]}

Research accounting:

- Plausible documents discovered: approximately 43 after deduplication.
- Underlying pages/PDFs opened: 20.
- Accepted new evidence units: 10 total; 8 final-evidence and 2 context.
- Reused evidence: 10 supplied records, including the identity/authority baseline, National Guard reform, electricity-reform constraint, Supreme Court review, Ayotzinapa process, and press-freedom material. These were not counted as newly discovered.
- Distinct organizations represented in new evidence: 9.
- Source types: official presidential/security records, official diplomatic record, UN/OHCHR, IACHR/OAS, NGO reports, government human-rights report, and international press analysis.

Lens coverage:

- 2B.1: supported, with diplomatic de-escalation and neutrality evidence.
- 2B.2: residual gap. Targeted searches found no evidence that Mexico initiated or expanded a war, cross-border coercive campaign, annexation, or foreign proxy conflict in 2022.
- 2B.3: partially supported through security-rationale claims, homicide trends, and contrary militarization assessments.
- 2B.4: partially supported; evidence concerns domestic security operations rather than conventional foreign military operations.
- 2B.5: supported concerning foreseeable risks, military control, alleged abuses, and accountability.
- 2B.6: partially supported; official security claims and Ukraine rhetoric are documented, but systematic misinformation attribution remains unresolved.
- 2B.7: supported by the Ukraine peace and mediation initiative; no achieved ceasefire was found.
- 2B.8: partially supported by large security-force deployments and militarization; detailed 2022 spending attribution was access-blocked.
- 2B.9: partially supported; accountability failures are documented, but personal acceptance of specific military failures is not established.
- 2B.10: partially supported; external diplomacy was generally peace-oriented, while domestic militarization and strained Ukraine-related relations provide contrary evidence.

Manifest update: writing the cumulative `research-ledger-manifest.json` failed because the workspace filesystem was read-only and the shell helper also encountered a sandbox loopback error. The cumulative manifest therefore remains represented here by the supplied prior index plus `WEB-2B-001` through `WEB-2B-010`; no prior entries were intentionally replaced.

Residual access blockers included the SIPRI 2022 PDF timeout, several gob.mx pages blocked by 403/robots restrictions, and inaccessible UN/OAS press-release endpoints. Another Chapter 2B-specific search wave would have limited material value. The main remaining gaps are the absence of a Mexico-initiated foreign conflict, detailed military-spending attribution, and a stronger personal nexus for accountability; additional searching is unlikely to materially resolve these without new archival or legal records.
